  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to utilize a divide-and-conquer strategy combined with randomized search to navigate the complex structure of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The approach focuses on generating diverse candidate solutions through geometric progression in both frequency and magnitude spaces, then applying sparse optimization techniques to enhance these candidates. This differs from gradient-based methods by explicitly leveraging the problem's symmetry and structure through mathematical transformation, offering potential breakthroughs while managing computational cost through intelligent sampling.\n\nKey limitations addressed:\n- Avoids local optima by combining randomization with deterministic optimization\n- Ensures boundary preservation by mathematical transformation\n- Balances exploration and exploitation through adaptive sampling\n- Maintains exact integration by linear interpolation\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe problem involves finding a step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes:\n$$ C_5 = \\max_k \\left| \\int \\mathrm{h}(x) \\left(1-\\mathrm{h}(x+k)\\right) \\mathrm{d}x \\right| $$\nwhich is discretized as:\n$$ C_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\mathrm{np.correlate}(\\mathbf{h}, 1-\\mathbf{h}, \\text{mode='full'})[k] \\cdot \\Delta x \\right) $$\n\nWith $\\Delta x = 2/n$.\n\nRepresentation: Piecewise constant function defined on n grid points.\n\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f\n\nConstraints:\n- $\\sum h_i = n/2$\n- $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$\n\nObjective: Minimize maximum normalized overlap correlation.\n\nFormulation: We reduced the problem to sparse optimization by defining score functions $s_{ij} = - \\log \\theta(\\mathrm{h}[i], \\mathrm{h}[j])$  \nwhere $\\theta$ is a threshold function, then express the problem as:\n\n$$ \\min_{\\mathbf{h}\\in[0,1]^{n}} \\max_{k} s_{ik} \\quad \\text{subject to} \\quad \\sum_{i} h_i = n/2 $$\n\nSolver selection:\n- CVXPY for the sparse relaxation subproblems\n- SLSQP for local refinement\n- Comb of both for end-to-end optimization\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nDefine function signatures:\n```python\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n    import numpy as np\n    import time\n    from scipy.optimize import minimize\n    import cvxpy as cp\n\n    # Set up parameters and initial attempt\n    parent_n = kwargs.get('parent_n', None) or 300\n    deadline = time.time() + budget_s\n\n    # Generate initial candidate\n    h0 = parent_n % 2 == 0 and np.full(parent_n, 0.5)\n    if h0 is None and time.time() < deadline - 10:\n        h0 = generate_periodic_parent(parent_n)\n        \n    # Transform to continuous space\n    h_scaled = scale_to_feasible(np.array(initial_values) if parent_n is None else h0)\n\n    # Find active transformation matrix (local case)\n    transform = generate_transformation(parent_n)\n\n    # Phase 1: Sparse optimization over angular space\n    best_c = float('inf')\n    best_sol = h_scaled\n\n    # Sample theta from geometric distribution\n    thetas = np.exp(np.linspace(-3, 0.5, 50))\n\n    for theta in thetas:\n        if time.time() >= deadline - 5:\n            break\n            \n        # Sample frequency space\n        freq = np.exp(np.log(np.linspace(1.0, 5.0, 100))\n        \n        # Randomized sparse search\n        center = np.exp(np.log(np.random.randn(100)) * 0.7)\n        candidates = center + np.exp(np.log(np.linspace(-3,1,50))\n        \n        # Use sparse solver\n        sparse_sol = solve_sparse_transform(transform, h_scaled, theta)\n        new_c = evaluate_overlap(sparse_sol)\n        \n        if new_c < best_c and is_feasible(sparse_sol):\n            best_c = new_c\n            best_sol = sparse_sol\n\n    # Phase 2: Local refinement with CVXPY\n    final_sol = minimize_overlap(best_sol, deadline - time.time())\n    final_c = evaluate_overlap(final_sol)\n\n    # Return final solution\n    return final_sol, final_c, parent_n\n```\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nComplete configuration:\n- Primary search space: Logarithmic space from 1e-3 to 5.0\n- Secondary search: Phase space with frequency/brightness parameters\n- Tertiary adjustments: Blind search in 1-parameter space\n\nUseful variations:\nVariation 1: Random mutation\n  Approx. 70% of budget\n  Changes: Random slices transformed by rotation matrix\n\nVariation 2: Sparse optimization\n  Approx. 20% of budget\n  Finds optimal regularized solutions\n\nVariation 3: Cluster detection\n  Probabilistic early termination of low-potential paths\n\nCarry over only returned optimal solution from each phase.\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness\nDominant costs:\n- Sparse transformation: 60% of time\n- Phase 3 local optimization: 35%\n- Exploration: 5%\n\nExecution schedule:\n1. Compute core elements (tree)\n2. Launch wavefronts (rays) simultaneously\n3. Detect promising regions promptly\n4. Terminal extraction when time exceeds 20%\n\nBudget recovery: Use trajectory memory for quick fallback\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nDefine checks:\n- End-to-end objectivation using compute_all_lags\n- Second derivative check (local curvature)\n- Variation plateaus detected by LSH\n- Automated file validator\n\nReturn format: standard tuple format\n\nCompliance checks:\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1\n- Sum(h) = n/2\n- Finiteness of returned C_5\n- Epsilon < 0.3808 selected metrics\n\nFallback recovery: Return parent solution if optimization fails",
